The set of good squares is $\{1, \dots, N\} \setminus S_{bad}$.
Since $S_{bad}$ is a union of disjoint intervals $[L_i, R_i]$, the good squares are also a union of disjoint intervals.
Specifically, the good squares are:
$[1, L_1 - 1]$, $[R_1 + 1, L_2 - 1]$, ..., $[R_M + 1, N]$.
Wait, the problem says $1 < L_i$, so $1$ is always good. Also $R_i < N$, so $N$ is always good.
So the good squares are indeed a union of intervals.

Let's consider the intervals of good squares.
Let these intervals be $G_1, G_2, \dots, G_k$.
$G_1 = [1, L_1 - 1]$.
$G_2 = [R_1 + 1, L_2 - 1]$.
...
$G_{M+1} = [R_M + 1, N]$.
Note that if $L_i = R_i + 1$, then the gap is empty, but the problem says $R_i < L_{i+1}$, so there is at least one integer between $R_i$ and $L_{i+1}$? No, $R_i < L_{i+1}$ means $L_{i+1} \ge R_i + 1$. So $[R_i+1, L_{i+1}-1]$ is a valid interval (possibly empty if $L_{i+1} = R_i + 1$).
Actually, the condition $R_i < L_{i+1}$ ensures that the bad intervals are disjoint.
