The problem asks us to find the number of connected simple undirected graphs with $N$ vertices and $M$ edges such that the number of vertices at an even shortest distance from vertex 1 is equal to the number of vertices at an odd shortest distance from vertex 1. We need to output this count modulo $P$ for all $M$ from $N-1$ to $\frac{N(N-1)}{2}$. The constraints are $N \le 30$ and $P$ is a prime around $10^9$.

Let $d(v)$ be the shortest distance from vertex 1 to vertex $v$ in the graph. The condition is that $|\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}| = |\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}|$. Since there are $N$ vertices in total, this implies that exactly $N/2$ vertices have even distance and $N/2$ vertices have odd distance. Note that $d(1) = 0$, which is even. So vertex 1 is always counted in the even set.

Let's analyze the condition. The vertices can be partitioned into two sets based on the parity of their distance from vertex 1: $V_{even} = \{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}$ and $V_{odd} = \{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}$. The condition is $|V_{even}| = |V_{odd}| = N/2$.
Since $d(1)=0$, $1 \in V_{even}$.
Also, if there is an edge between $u$ and $v$, then $|d(u) - d(v)| \le 1$.
Specifically, if $u \in V_{even}$ and $v \in V_{odd}$, then $d(v) \le d(u) + 1$ and $d(u) \le d(v) + 1$. This is consistent.
If there is an edge between two vertices in $V_{even}$, say $u, v \in V_{even}$, then $d(u)$ is even and $d(v)$ is even. The triangle inequality says $|d(u) - d(v)| \le 1$. Since both are even, $|d(u) - d(v)|$ must be 0. So $d(u) = d(v)$. This doesn't immediately lead to a contradiction, but usually, in a bipartite graph, edges only go between $V_{even}$ and $V_{odd}$.
Wait, if the graph is bipartite, then all edges connect a vertex in $V_{even}$ to a vertex in $V_{odd}$. In that case, the condition $|V_{even}| = |V_{odd}|$ is just a condition on the size of the bipartition.
However, the graph is not necessarily bipartite. If there is an edge between two vertices in $V_{even}$, say $u, v$, then $d(u)$ and $d(v)$ are even. The shortest path from 1 to $u$ has length $d(u)$, and to $v$ has length $d(v)$. If there is an edge $(u, v)$, then $d(v) \le d(u) + 1$ and $d(u) \le d(v) + 1$. If $d(u) \neq d(v)$, say $d(u) < d(v)$, then $d(v) \le d(u) + 1$. Since $d(u)$ is even, $d(u)+1$ is odd. But $d(v)$ is even. So $d(v) \le d(u) + 1$ implies $d(v) = d(u) + 1$ is impossible. Thus $d(v) \le d(u)$ is not possible if $d(u) < d(v)$. Wait.
