The problem asks us to calculate the sum of $f(P)$ over all permutations $P$ of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$, modulo 998244353.
Here, $f(P)$ is formed by concatenating the decimal representations of the numbers in the permutation $P$.
Let $P = (P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N)$.
$f(P)$ is the integer obtained by concatenating $P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N$.
We need to compute $\sum_{P} f(P) \pmod{998244353}$.

Let's analyze the contribution of each digit to the total sum.
The value of a number formed by concatenating strings $S_1, S_2, \ldots, S_N$ is $\sum_{i=1}^N S_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N \text{len}(S_j)}$, where $\text{len}(S)$ is the number of digits in $S$.
In our case, the strings $S_i$ are the decimal representations of the numbers $P_i$.
Let $L(x)$ be the number of digits in $x$.
Then $f(P) = \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L(P_j)}$.

We want to compute $\sum_{P} \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L(P_j)}$.
By linearity of expectation (or just swapping sums), this is equal to:
$\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{P} P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L(P_j)}$.

Let's fix the position $i$ in the permutation. We want to sum over all permutations $P$ the term $P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L(P_j)}$.
The term $P_i$ depends on which number is placed at position $i$.
The term $10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L(P_j)}$ depends on the lengths of the numbers placed at positions $i+1, \ldots, N$.

Let's consider the set of available numbers $\{1, 2, \ldots, N\}$.
Let $cnt[k]$ be the count of numbers in $\{1, \ldots, N\}$ that have exactly $k$ digits.
Since $N \le 2 \times 10^5$, the maximum number of digits is 6 (since $10^5$ has 6 digits, $2 \times 10^5$ has 6 digits).
Actually, $N$ can be up to $200,000$, so the numbers are up to $200,000$.
The number of digits $L(x)$ can be 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.
Specifically:
- 1 digit: $1, \ldots, 9$ (9 numbers)
- 2 digits: $10, \ldots, 99$ (90 numbers)
- 3 digits: $100, \ldots, 999$ (900 numbers)
- ...
- $k$ digits: $10^{k-1}, \ldots, 10^k - 1$.
- For $N=200000$, we have numbers up to $200000$.
  - 1 digit: $1..9$ (9)
  - 2 digits: $10..99$ (90)
  - 3 digits: $100..999$ (900)
  - 4 digits: $1000..9999$ (9000)
  - 5 digits: $10000..99999$ (90000)
  - 6 digits: $100000..200000$ (100001)
  Wait, $100000$ to $200000$ is $200000 - 100000 + 1 = 100001$.
  Total numbers: $9+90+90
